[[:db/retract 17592186163840 :answer-field/choices 17592186163841] [:db/retract 17592186163840 :answer-field/choices 17592186163842] [:db/retract 17592186163840 :answer-field/choices 17592186163843] [:db/retract 17592186163840 :answer-field/choices 17592186163844] [:db/retract 17592186163840 :answer-field/choices 17592186163845] {:db/id 17592186167127 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F$ and the surface $S$ with unit normal vector $n,$ where\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=⟨2z,\\,x+y,\\,y-z⟩\\,,\\,n(x,y,z)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨1,\\,-1,\\,1⟩\\,.\n$$\n\nIf the flux of $F$ across $S$ with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}{∬}_{S}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nCalculating the dot product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨2z,\\,x+y,\\,y-z⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨1,\\,-1,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨2z,\\,x+y,\\,y-z⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\,-1,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}(2z-(x+y)+(y-z)) \\\\  & =\\frac{z-x}{\\sqrt{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\frac{z-x}{\\sqrt{3}}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}{∬}_{S}z-x\\,\\text{d}S.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186163840 :answer-field/correct "answer-ad810e29-dc47-543e-aeb1-8297ce882407" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a0cc803d-28d2-5c8a-ad4d-e88ee6c149ca" "answer-51f5f2fd-94a6-51c3-ac48-99d59a93e907" "answer-e9761621-383d-53ec-8615-e110f65c1ad5" "answer-8524a039-125d-5c28-8aaa-2b417ce25d91" "answer-ad810e29-dc47-543e-aeb1-8297ce882407"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a0cc803d-28d2-5c8a-ad4d-e88ee6c149ca" :answer/id #uuid "a0cc803d-28d2-5c8a-ad4d-e88ee6c149ca" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-y-z" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-51f5f2fd-94a6-51c3-ac48-99d59a93e907" :answer/id #uuid "51f5f2fd-94a6-51c3-ac48-99d59a93e907" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x+2y+z" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e9761621-383d-53ec-8615-e110f65c1ad5" :answer/id #uuid "e9761621-383d-53ec-8615-e110f65c1ad5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x+y+2z" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8524a039-125d-5c28-8aaa-2b417ce25d91" :answer/id #uuid "8524a039-125d-5c28-8aaa-2b417ce25d91" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y+z" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ad810e29-dc47-543e-aeb1-8297ce882407" :answer/id #uuid "ad810e29-dc47-543e-aeb1-8297ce882407" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "z-x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186166882 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-627a222e-9363-5b8b-b1ec-0730c89cfcbe" "question-2ae7b222-1017-5e06-9f85-8323117b3184"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-627a222e-9363-5b8b-b1ec-0730c89cfcbe" :question/id #uuid "627a222e-9363-5b8b-b1ec-0730c89cfcbe" :question/math-academy-id "q-161193" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨z,\\,y,\\,x⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z=x+y$ for $0\\le x\\le 3$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$ Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S$ with respect to the upward-pointing unit normal vector $n.$\n\n*Hint: You're given that the fundamental vector product of $S$ with respect to its canonical parametrization is $||{r}_{x}\\times {r}_{y}||=\\sqrt{3}.$*" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that our surface $z=x+y$ can be written using the dot product as \n\n$$\n⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨-1,\\,-1,\\,1⟩}_{⏟}}_{\\overset{~}{n}}=0,\\,\\text{or}\\,⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨1,\\,1,\\,-1⟩}_{⏟}}_{-\\overset{~}{n}}=0.\n$$\n\nSince we require an upward-pointing normal vector to our surface $S,$ we must select one where the $z$-component is positive. This gives us the normal vector\n\n$$\n\\overset{~}{n}=⟨-1,\\,-1,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nNormalizing this vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨-1,\\,-1,\\,1⟩}{\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨-1,\\,-1,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=x+y$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨z,\\,y,\\,x⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨-1,\\,-1,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨x+y,\\,y,\\,x⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,\\,-1,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}(-(x+y)-y+x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}(-2y) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}\\,y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}(-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}\\,y)\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}\\,y)\\cdot ||{r}_{x}\\times {r}_{y}||\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}y\\cdot \\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-\\frac{2}{\\cancel{\\sqrt{3}}}y\\cdot \\cancel{\\sqrt{3}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-2y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{3}-2y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{1}2y\\,\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-[{y}^{2}]_{0}^{1}\\cdot [x]_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =-1\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5c6a2aa9-7d14-5b93-966f-5ef76762e118"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5c6a2aa9-7d14-5b93-966f-5ef76762e118" :answer-field/id #uuid "5c6a2aa9-7d14-5b93-966f-5ef76762e118" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-c73e0516-826a-57fd-99ad-0e6ddc957f95" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a5039a5b-f9d4-5a06-b32c-4be440d0e059" "answer-2e7ca8ec-891f-5ccc-985b-4e4a85531ac2" "answer-c73e0516-826a-57fd-99ad-0e6ddc957f95" "answer-f817a54c-d2b8-5a8f-9b28-dbefeb579916" "answer-3ccaaf5e-0e10-5c99-a0bc-d8a834cb86f3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a5039a5b-f9d4-5a06-b32c-4be440d0e059" :answer/id #uuid "a5039a5b-f9d4-5a06-b32c-4be440d0e059" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2e7ca8ec-891f-5ccc-985b-4e4a85531ac2" :answer/id #uuid "2e7ca8ec-891f-5ccc-985b-4e4a85531ac2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c73e0516-826a-57fd-99ad-0e6ddc957f95" :answer/id #uuid "c73e0516-826a-57fd-99ad-0e6ddc957f95" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f817a54c-d2b8-5a8f-9b28-dbefeb579916" :answer/id #uuid "f817a54c-d2b8-5a8f-9b28-dbefeb579916" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3ccaaf5e-0e10-5c99-a0bc-d8a834cb86f3" :answer/id #uuid "3ccaaf5e-0e10-5c99-a0bc-d8a834cb86f3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-2ae7b222-1017-5e06-9f85-8323117b3184" :question/id #uuid "2ae7b222-1017-5e06-9f85-8323117b3184" :question/math-academy-id "q-162158" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨z+5,\\,x-2y,\\,3x-2y⟩$ and a finite region $S$ of the plane $z=x+2y+1$ such that $\\text{Area}(S)=\\sqrt{2}.$ Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S$ with respect to the *downward*-pointing unit normal vector $n.$" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that our surface $z=x+2y+1$ can be written using the dot product as \n\n$$\n⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨-1,\\,-2,\\,1⟩}_{⏟}}_{\\overset{~}{n}}=1,\\,\\text{or}\\,⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩\\cdot {{⟨1,\\,2,\\,-1⟩}_{⏟}}_{-\\overset{~}{n}}=-1.\n$$\n\nSince we require a downward-pointing normal vector to our surface $S,$ we must select one where the $z$-component is negative. This gives us the normal vector\n\n$$\n\\overset{~}{n}=⟨1,\\,2,\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nNormalizing this vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨1,\\,2,\\,-1⟩}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨1,\\,2,\\,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=x+2y+1$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨z+5,\\,x-2y,\\,3x-2y⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨1,\\,2,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}⟨x+2y+6,\\,x-2y,\\,3x-2y⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\,2,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}((x+2y+6)+2(x-2y)-(3x-2y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}(x+2y+6+2x-4y-3x+2y) \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\sqrt{6}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}\\cdot \\text{Area}(S) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}\\cdot \\sqrt{2} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-6ac4cbf3-d6da-5e60-8eb4-cf067330135d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-6ac4cbf3-d6da-5e60-8eb4-cf067330135d" :answer-field/id #uuid "6ac4cbf3-d6da-5e60-8eb4-cf067330135d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-f6e593f7-c2c1-5fc5-8a3d-c699694f0c1d" :answer-field/choices 